  "subject": "🇳🇵Axios Finish Line: Nepali heroes",
  "date": "Wed, 02 Sep 2026 21:28:43 -0400",
  "body_text": "96 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n 🍯 Plus: Honeymoon in the parks | Wednesday, September 02, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n View in browser \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n PRESENTED BY PEPSICO \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n Axios Finish Line \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n By Mike Allen and Erica Pandey and Jim VandeHei &middot; Sep 02, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n Welcome back! Smart Brevity™ count: 398 words ... 1½ min. Copy edited by Sheryl Miller. \n\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 1 big thing: Rescues from ruin in Nepal \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n A woman walks among the ruins of flash floods in Nepal's Nuwakot district yesterday. Photo: Arun Sankar/AFP via Getty Images The scale of suffering and death after last week's terrifying flash floods in Nepal and Tibet is almost impossible to comprehend, writes Axios' Erica Pandey, who's the daughter of Nepali immigrants. \n\n Yet amid the despair are extraordinary acts of heroism — rescues that felt like miracles and families reunited against the odds. Here are three stories that bring us hope: 🎒 Rajendra Dawadi was inside his classroom when a colleague rushed in with a warning.\n\n Within minutes, Dawadi rang the school bell and ordered his students and staff out of the building and toward higher ground. He also called bus drivers who were driving in with more students and told them to turn around, AP reports . Rajendra Dawadi. Photo: Rajesh Kumar Singh/AP As Dawadi followed students and staff to a nearby hill, the floodwaters rose behind him. From the safety of the hillside, he turned to watch the school where he had spent years teaching — and where he had once been a student — disappear beneath a torrent of water, mud, boulders and debris.\n\n Dawadi saved at least 900 students on campus, plus many more who were on their way to school. 🚁 Priya Adhikari — Nepal's first female helicopter captain — has flown 100+ rescue missions and counting since the floods hit.\n\n \"We are trained for this,\" she told CNN's Anderson Cooper , referring to the skill required to pilot helicopters through Nepal's mountainous terrain and narrow valleys. \"The smile I have is for the survivors,\" she continued. \"The rescues that I'm doing, people lying down inside my helicopter, trying to open their eyes. I smiled at them and say, 'You survived.'\" 💛 Matti Maya Tamang, a mom of two, thought she'd lost both of her boys when floods tore through their school.\n\n But Zoyal Ghale, her 8-year-old, had saved himself by running to the woods and climbing a tree. Reuters journalist Sahana Bajracharya, who had spoken with Ghale as he was airlifted to safety, helped reconnect him with his mother. Tamang had already found her younger son, and the family was reunited in Kathmandu. The boys' father, who works construction in Japan, is also headed home to Nepal, Tamang told Reuters. Watch a video . \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\nA MESSAGE FROM PEPSICO\r\n\n \r\n Human-first AI starts in the field \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n PepsiCo says AI adoption gets easier when tools work for farmers’ crops, land, climates and decision windows.\n\n\r\n The strategy: Pair technology with trusted agronomists, local context and clear economic value.\n\n\r\n Read the Q&amp;A. \n\n\r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 2. 🏞️ Parting shot! \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Photo: Joe Capraro Finish Line readers and newlyweds Madi and Joe Capraro are on their honeymoon through the national parks. This stunner is from their first stop, Grand Teton National Park.\n\n Next up: Yellowstone. \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n 👆 Like this comms style and format? It's called Smart Brevity®. Bring it to your org — via hands-on training or internal comms software — to harness its power and impact. \r\n &nbsp; Axios thanks our partners for supporting our journalism.\n Advertise with us. \n\n Read more about Axios' mission , ethics and how we use AI in our journalism . You can reach the authors by replying to this email.\nSponsorship has no influence on editorial content.\n\n Thank you for signing up for this Axios newsletter. To stop receiving this newsletter, unsubscribe or manage your email preferences . If someone forwarded this to you, sign up now to get Axios in your inbox.\n\n Axios, PO Box 101060, Arlington VA 22201 &nbsp; Follow Axios across:\n\n &nbsp; &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;",
