/tr><tr>\r\n<td height=\"10\" style=\"height:10px;font-size:0;border-bottom:10px solid #f5f5f5\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr>      <tr>\r\n<td height=\"10\" style=\"height:10px;font-size:0\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr><tr>\r\n<td style=\"padding:20px 3.5% 10px;line-height:1.5\">\r\n<span class=\"b k\" style=\"font-size:27px;font-family:'gorditamedium',Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;color:#222;letter-spacing:-0.1px;line-height:1.5\">  1 big thing: Rescues from ruin in Nepal </span>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>   <tr>\r\n<td height=\"10\" style=\"height:10px;font-size:0\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr> <tr>\r\n<td class=\"v\" style=\"padding:0 3.5%;line-height:1.75;font-size:17px;font-family:Georgia,serif;color:#222;letter-spacing:-0.1px\"><div align=\"center\"><!--[if mso]><img alt='' src='https://images.axios.com/pGM7uWhyCqzIVTvdX7UGvWrxJ8A=/fit-in/1116x1300/2026/09/02/1788377960707.jpeg' style='display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;' width='558' /><![endif]--><!--[if !mso]><!--><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://images.axios.com/pGM7uWhyCqzIVTvdX7UGvWrxJ8A=/fit-in/1116x1300/2026/09/02/1788377960707.jpeg\" style=\"display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;\" width=\"100%\"/><!--<![endif]--></div> <div class=\"bodytext\" style=\"font-size:13px; font-family:'Gordita',Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif; color:#848484; margin-bottom:20px;\">A woman walks among the ruins of flash floods in Nepal's Nuwakot district yesterday. Photo: Arun Sankar/AFP via Getty Images</div><p><strong>The scale of suffering</strong> and death after last week's terrifying flash floods in Nepal and Tibet is almost impossible to comprehend, <em>writes Axios' Erica Pandey, who's the daughter of Nepali immigrants.</em></p><ul><li><strong>Yet amid the despair</strong> are extraordinary acts of heroism — rescues that felt like miracles and families reunited against the odds. Here are three stories that bring us hope:</li></ul><p><strong>🎒 Rajendra Dawadi</strong> was inside his classroom when a colleague rushed in with a warning.</p><ul><li><strong>Within minutes, </strong>Dawadi rang the school bell and ordered his students and staff out of the building and toward higher ground. He also called bus drivers who were driving in with more students and told them to turn around, <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47316523.\" target=\"_blank\"><em>AP reports</em></a><em>.</em></li></ul><div align=\"center\"><!--[if mso]><img alt='' src='https://images.axios.com/JE_xdwpkiJk4dETM69varsjPHL8=/fit-in/1116x1300/2026/09/02/1788377661623.jpeg' style='display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;' width='558' /><![endif]--><!--[if !mso]><!--><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://images.axios.com/JE_xdwpkiJk4dETM69varsjPHL8=/fit-in/1116x1300/2026/09/02/1788377661623.jpeg\" style=\"display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;\" width=\"100%\"/><!--<![endif]--></div> <div class=\"bodytext\" style=\"font-size:13px; font-family:'Gordita',Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif; color:#848484; margin-bottom:20px;\">Rajendra Dawadi. Photo: Rajesh Kumar Singh/AP</div><p><strong>As Dawadi followed </strong>students and staff to a nearby hill, the floodwaters rose behind him. From the safety of the hillside, he turned to watch the school where he had spent years teaching — and where he had once been a student — disappear beneath a torrent of water, mud, boulders and debris.</p><ul><li><strong>Dawadi saved at least </strong>900 students on campus, plus many more who were on their way to school.</li></ul><p><strong>🚁 Priya Adhikari </strong>— Nepal's first female helicopter captain —<strong> </strong>has flown 100+ rescue missions and counting since the floods hit.</p><ul><li><strong>\"We are trained for this,\"</strong> she<a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47316523.435378/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuY25uLmNvbS8yMDI2LzA4LzMxL3dvcmxkL3ZpZGVvL25lcGFsLWZlbWFsZS1oZWxpY29wdGVyLXBpbG90LWRpZ3ZpZC1obms_dXRtX3NvdXJjZT1uZXdzbGV0dGVyJnV0bV9tZWRpdW09ZW1haWwmdXRtX2NhbXBhaWduPW5ld3NsZXR0ZXJfYXhpb3NmaW5pc2hsaW5lJnN0cmVhbT10b3A/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aBbcf252bf\" target=\"_blank\"> told CNN's Anderson Cooper</a>, referring to the skill required to pilot helicopters through Nepal's mountainous terrain and narrow valleys.</li><li><strong>\"The smile I have </strong>is for the survivors,\" she continued. \"The rescues that I'm doing, people lying down inside my helicopter, trying to open their eyes. I smiled at them and say, 'You survived.'\"</li></ul><p><strong>💛 Matti Maya Tamang, </strong>a mom of two, thought she'd lost both of her boys when floods tore through their school.</p><ul><li><strong>But Zoyal Ghale,</strong> her 8-year-old, had saved himself by running to the woods and climbing a tree.</li><li><strong>Reuters journalist </strong>Sahana Bajracharya, who had spoken with Ghale as he was airlifted to safety, <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47316523.\" target=\"_blank\">helped reconnect him</a> with his mother. Tamang had already found her younger son, and the family was reunited in Kathmandu. The boys' father, who works construction in Japan, is also headed home to Nepal, Tamang told Reuters. <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47316523.435378/aHR0cHM6Ly94LmNvbS9SZXV0ZXJzL3N0YXR1cy8yMDkzNDYzNjM2MjM2OTY4MjQyP3V0bV9zb3VyY2U9bmV3c2xldHRlciZ1dG1fbWVkaXVtPWVtYWlsJnV0bV9jYW1wYWlnbj1uZXdzbGV0dGVyX2F4aW9zZmluaXNobGluZSZzdHJlYW09dG9w/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aBcdc9e7a6\" target=\"_blank\"><em>Watch a video</em></a><em>.</em></li></ul></td>\r\n</tr> <tr><td align=\"center\" style=\"padding:20px 3.5% 25px\">   <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47316523.435378/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuZmFjZWJvb2suY29tL3NoYXJlci5waHA_dT1odHRwczovL3d3dy5heGlvcy5jb20vbmV3c2xldHRlcnMvYXhpb3MtZmluaXNoLWxpbmUtNzhhOGM0MzAtYTcwNC0xMWYxLWFiMzMtNmY2OGViODk2NDE4Lmh0bWw_Y2h1bmslM0QxJTI2dXRtX3Rlcm0lM0RmYnNvY2lhbHNoYXJlJTIzc3RvcnkxJnV0bV9zb3VyY2U9bmV3c2xldHRlciZ1dG1fbWVkaXVtPWVtYWlsJnV0bV9jYW1wYWlnbj1uZXdzbGV0dGVyX2F4aW9zZmluaXNobGluZSZzdHJlYW09dG9w/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aBc3d2cf85\" style=\"display:inline-block\"><img width=\"32\" height=\"32\" style=\"bo